1
00:00:03,212 --> 00:00:06,903
Enabling a feature adds its pages to the admin menu.

2
00:00:06,903 --> 00:00:12,312
The server's settings are under Settings, OpenID Connect, Authorization Server.

3
00:00:12,772 --> 00:00:17,641
A warning at the top says that saving these settings might restart the website.

4
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:23,312
The settings follow in groups, starting with Endpoints, the addresses that applications call.

5
00:00:23,763 --> 00:00:26,928
Flows are the ways an application can get tokens.

6
00:00:26,928 --> 00:00:31,532
By default, the Authorization Code Flow and the Refresh Token Flow are on,

7
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:33,603
and every endpoint is enabled.

8
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:39,975
Further down are the groups Proof Key for Code Exchange and Pushed Authorization Requests.

9
00:00:39,975 --> 00:00:45,150
The last group, Advanced Options, is marked as intended for advanced use only.

10
00:00:45,612 --> 00:00:50,262
Applications are under Access Control, OpenID Connect, Applications.

11
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:55,352
An application is a program that is registered to ask this site for tokens.

12
00:00:55,352 --> 00:00:57,992
The list is empty until you add one.

13
00:00:58,449 --> 00:01:00,432
Scopes are in the same menu.

14
00:01:00,432 --> 00:01:04,455
A scope names a kind of access that an application can request.

15
00:01:04,455 --> 00:01:07,429
The list has Email, Phone, Profile and Roles.

16
00:01:09,029 --> 00:01:13,810
Each feature adds its own pages to the admin menu when it is enabled.

17
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:21,013
Under Settings, OpenID Connect, these are Authorization Server, Token Validation and Authentication Client.

18
00:01:21,013 --> 00:01:25,539
Applications and Scopes are under Access Control, OpenID Connect.

